The transformation is a Curtius rearrangement of an isocyanate to an isothiocyanate. The substrate contains a nitro-substituted cyclohexyl isocyanate, while the product is the corresponding cyclohexyl isothiocyanate. The key change is replacement of the N–H bond by an N–S bond, with the nitro group remaining unchanged.

A polar aprotic solvent is appropriate because it dissolves the organic substrate and supports the ionic intermediates formed during the rearrangement. N-Methyl-2-pyrrolidone is well suited because it is a moderately polar, aprotic, and non-nucleophilic medium that is compatible with the acidic conditions of the reaction.

