The transformation is a Corey–Fuchs-type cyclization of an N-acyl anthranilic acid derivative. The key change is formation of the fused N–H bond between the amide nitrogen and the aromatic ring, converting the carboxylic acid into a cyclic imide while leaving the chloro-substituted fused ring unchanged.

A polar aprotic medium is appropriate because it dissolves the organic substrate and supports the nucleophilic acyl-substitution process. N,N-Diethylacetamide is well suited because it is compatible with the amide and aryl chloride functionalities, and its N–H group can participate in the cyclization without competing for the amide carbonyl.

